"Dayboard is a new, public, open-source project; this is its first piece of work. Work in space `dayboard`. The code will live in the two child repositories, dayboard-api (Python) and dayboard-web (TanStack Start); this workspace holds the records.\n\nTHE IDEA\nA daily page for one person and their AI assistant. Each day has a page with a title and blocks of fixed kinds: schedule (meetings), open items, pull requests, learnings, waiting on others, and notes for tomorrow. A block holds items; an item has text, optional links, and a state the assistant and the person can change (open, done, delayed to tomorrow). The assistant writes most of the page after investigating the person's tools; the person reads it during the day. The page always opens on the latest day on record, today or later.\n\nNotes for tomorrow belong to each day's page: the assistant fills them on the person's request (\"delay this to tomorrow\"), the page shows them, and the assistant reads them when it prepares the next day, by a list of dates, or the latest day on record when no dates are given (the Friday page when asked on Monday).\n\nAny assistant reaches the same data through an MCP server: list days, read a page, write or update blocks and items, read notes by dates. No authentication. A \"copy prompt\" button on the page puts on the clipboard the instruction the person pastes into their assistant to update today's page through the MCP server.\n\nWHO IT IS FOR\nMe first: today my assistant writes my daily report as a chat artifact and keeps its own memory of it. Dayboard replaces that with a real page, better design, fewer tokens, and it works from any assistant. Later, other people and their own kinds of blocks.\n\nWHAT IS FIXED\n- One person. No users, no user field, no configuration of one; every record belongs to whoever runs it. Users are a later piece of work.\n- Runs locally: one command for the API, one for the web app. Designed so it can run on Cloudflare or anywhere else later without a rewrite, but not now.\n- Public repositories: nothing company-specific, no internal names, no secrets, no references to my employer's tools or processes. Dayboard is project-agnostic; the block kinds are generic.\n- Interfaces must look current: a type scale, spacing, visual hierarchy, considered states, light and dark, phone width.\n\nSTACK PROPOSAL\n- Backend in Python. Frontend in TanStack Start with TanStack Query, and TanStack Form where a form exists. Few dependencies.\n- Storage, two proposals to decide between later with the trade-off written down: plain files in the directory the service runs from, git-ignored, one file per day; or SQLite, one file, whose schema moves to Cloudflare D1 later. Simplicity for the current goal decides.\n\nOUT OF SCOPE FOR NOW, BUT NOT TO BE DESIGNED OUT\nUsers and authentication, cloud deployment, custom block kinds added by users, a plugin or engine layer, notifications, any integration that reads the person's tools directly (the assistant does that)."
